tial equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y=0,\n$$\n\nwe have that if\n\n$$\n{{field-1}}=g(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $g(x)$ is independent of $y,$ then an integrating factor for the equation is\n\n$$\nI(x)={{field-2}}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\operatorname{exp}⁡(x)$ denotes the exponential function ${e}^{x}.$\n\nLet's denote\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=2xy,\\,Q(x,y)=1.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}={{field-3}}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}={{field-4}}\n$$\n\nSince for this equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{Q}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}-\\frac{∂Q}{∂x})={{field-5}},\n$$\n\nwhich depends on $x$ only, then an integrating factor for our equation is\n\n$$\nI(x)={{field-6}},\n$$\n\nand we arrive at the exact equation\n\n$$\n{{field-7}}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069857/state.json" "file_sha256" "ff78b0778dd4a17d5bed3e2fb18bcca075ccb3c3093b0045d7201edbbea16600"} {"question" "q-337400" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a differential equation of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y=0,\n$$\n\nwe have that if\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{Q}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}-\\frac{∂Q}{∂x})=g(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $g(x)$ is independent of $y,$ then an integrating factor for the equation is\n\n$$\nI(x)=\\operatorname{exp}⁡(\\int g(x)\\,\\text{d}x).\n$$\n\nLet's denote\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=2xy,\\,Q(x,y)=1.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2xy)=2x \\\\ \\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(1)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{Q}(\\frac{∂P}{∂y}-\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}) & =2x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis a function of $x$ only, we conclude that an integrating factor is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & =\\operatorname{exp}⁡(\\int 2x\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{exp}⁡({x}^{2}) \\\\  & ={e}^{{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the differential equation by the integrating f